{
  "result": "Every morning, a small bakery on the corner opens before the rest of the street has decided to wake. Its windows glow yellow against the blue dark, and the smell of bread escapes whenever the door swings open. People passing by slow down, even when they have nowhere to be.\n\nThe baker, whose name is Mara, keeps a notebook beside the register. It does not contain recipes. Instead, she writes down things customers say when they think nobody is listening: a child\u2019s invented word, a complaint about rain, a promise to call someone more often, a joke without a punchline.\n\nShe began the habit years ago, after realizing how quickly ordinary moments disappear. Great events announce themselves, demanding photographs and speeches. Smaller ones rarely ask to be remembered. Yet they accumulate around us like flour on a countertop, evidence of work and warmth that would otherwise go unnoticed each day.\n\nOne winter, a regular customer stopped coming. Months later, his daughter entered the bakery and mentioned that he had died. Mara found an old entry describing his excitement about a tomato he had grown on his balcony. She tore out the page and handed it to his daughter, who smiled.\n\nThe notebook is still there, though its cover has softened and its pages are uneven. Mara fills it slowly, between kneading dough and sweeping crumbs. She knows she cannot save every fleeting thing. Still, each morning, she opens the shop, uncaps her pen, and makes room for one more memory.",
